<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to BlockML's unifying Everything-is-a-Block principle

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.EverythingIsABlockPage-->
<docs:EverythingIsABlockPage xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:lrule="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.lib.rule">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to BlockML's unifying Everything-is-a-Block principle
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: the Everything-is-a-Block model, single inheritance root, and why one grammar covers
    domain and framework types, grounded in org.blockml.bml.documentation.EverythingIsABlock and
    org.blockml.bml.core.Block. Body content is authored in a04 from those sources.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:EverythingIsABlock" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — one root, one parser, one TypeRegistry bootstrap path.
    </see>
    <see type="core:Block" relation="Requires">
      Sole inheritance root every BlockML concept ultimately extends.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:CoreUseCases" relation="SeeAlso">
      What the principle is used for.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:BlockAndInstance" relation="ReadNext">
      Next reading step — definition versus instance.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Everything Is a Block</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <rules override="true">
      <composition>
        <lrule:CodeExamplePlacementRule />
        <lrule:CodeExplanationRule />
      </composition>
    </rules>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <!--1. Lead-->
        <dpage:Section heading="One root, one grammar">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="BlockML describes everything that belongs to its grammar as Blocks: there is one inheritance root, one parser, and one TypeRegistry bootstrap path." />
                <dpage:Text text="Branch-specific rules come from the baseType chain a Block declares, not from a separate, parallel ontology for each kind of thing." />
                <dpage:Text text="core:Block is that sole inheritance root, and every Block definition, whatever it describes, traces back to it." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--2. What counts as a Block-->
        <dpage:Section heading="What counts as a Block">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="Composable domain types, ValueTypes, Languages, Definition meta-model types, and documentation blocks are all Blocks — none of them needs a separate grammar or a parallel type system." />
                <dpage:Text text="Every one of them extends core:Block, either directly or through a branch." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:List>
              <items>
                <dpage:ListItem text="Composable domain types — domain-specific building blocks such as a Wall or a StandardBrick." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="ValueTypes — type definitions such as Integer or Decimal, Blocks that describe a value's nature, not the value itself." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="Languages — implementation language bindings such as TypeScript or OpenSCAD." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="Documentation blocks — narrative documentation, including this handbook's own pages, are Blocks like any other." />
              </items>
            </dpage:List>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--3. Two layers-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Authoring ontology versus compiler internals">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="In the authoring ontology, a Block is a named, definable unit with an FQN and a baseType chain — that is the mental model readers write against." />
                <dpage:Text text="Internally, the compiler is free to represent that information however is convenient, with no mandatory uniform runtime class hierarchy behind it." />
                <dpage:Text text="One consequence of this split shows up in ValueTypes: a ValueType never stores data itself — Properties own all concrete values, while the ValueType only describes the value's nature." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--4. Minimal example-->
        <dpage:Section heading="A domain Block and a ValueType, side by side">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="A domain Block and a ValueType look completely different in what they describe, but identical in how they are declared — both are root Block documents with a name, a type, and a baseType." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:CodeBlock>
              <code><![CDATA[<!-- A ValueType is declared exactly like any other Block — only its baseType marks it as a type definition rather than a domain instance -->
<acme:Temperature xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:acme="com.acme.example"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core">

  <baseType>
    core:ValueType
  </baseType>
</acme:Temperature>]]></code>
            </dpage:CodeBlock>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="Temperature's declaration is indistinguishable in shape from a composable domain Block: the same qualified root tag, the same single baseType field, nothing else." />
                <dpage:Text text="Nothing in that identity shape tells a domain Block and a ValueType apart; the distinction lives entirely in which baseType each one declares, not in a separate syntax." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--5. What to carry forward-->
        <dpage:Section heading="What to carry into the next pages">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="After this page, readers should be able to explain why domain types, ValueTypes, Languages, and documentation blocks are all Blocks, and expect every later page's syntax to build on that one shared grammar." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--6. Continuation-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with defining a Block">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Blocks" summary="Learn how to define, name, and reference a Block.">
                  <target>docs:Blocks</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:EverythingIsABlockPage>
